now the idea of a strict personal moral  code based on an unchanging set of  universal rules is seen by many as  old-fashioned and victorian  yet most of us retain the desire to be a  good person and morality is often cited  to support political and cultural  opinions and actions  so is this a hypocrisy designed to make  us look good but avoiding having to  change our own behavior  if we're to make use of the notion of  morality must we see morality as a  universal set of rules or law that  applies to all in all circumstances  or can we endorse a personal morality  that's sustained by the consistency and  integrity of the individual to give it  force and value  well fascinating topic and we've got  three really interesting speakers here  to debate it first of all jesse norman  is a treasury minister but he's here  because he's a keen political  philosopher and author of compassionate  conservatism as well as biographies of  adam smith and edmund burke  julie bindle is a radical feminist  writer and co-founder of the law reform  group justice for women which helps  women who've been prosecuted for killing  violent male partners  and jonathan wolfe who's known as joe is  a moral philosopher at oxford university  he's developing a research program on  revitalizing democracy and civil society  and his work largely concerns equality  social justice and poverty  so as usual i'm going to ask each of the  three to do a three-minute pitch on the  question of is it hypocritical to  believe that other cultures have  different moralities and yet think that  we can be objectively good jesse norman  first  i think from a purely philosophical  perspective uh the answer to your  question is that there isn't anything  hypocritical about  uh believing in universal morality as  regards one's own self  which of course may be a belief that's  uh  deluded in its own right and yet  thinking that uh other other moralities  are in some sense non-universal or  partial and the reason for that is that  people can be radically solidistic they  can  just believe that they're right  um and that their world is the one that  as it were is the only true world and  we've seen examples in history  uh the spanish inquisition comes to mind  in which people might have taken the  view that other people could have uh  local morality to their own kind but  there was only one morality that really  mattered so uh that's i suppose the  overall question but there's a there's a  deeper in a way more interesting issue  for me which is you know whether  there are such things as or there could  be such a thing as a universal a single  universal morality and uh it it's  sometimes uh easy to think that that  might be so because there's so much  moralizing and denunciation that seems  to go on at the moment on social media  and elsewhere and some of that is  directed uh  as it were  encouraging people to do the right thing  from all the best motives but quite a  lot of it seems to me about uh to be  about virtue signaling or advertising  one's own prowess and uh of course  anyone watching this might think that uh  you know politicians of all kinds are  amongst the worst uh uh offenders in  this area  so uh what is it that we think about  when you think what a reversal morality  well i would say that it's something  which is um  actually very hard to imagine uh not  only not existing  in this world uh and anyone who thinks  differently might want to think about  the limitations of the best candidates  for that cantianism or utilitarianism  uh a philosophy of duties or a  philosophy of uh as it were the best  advantage based on the the best outcome  for the for the greatest number or some  such a hedonic calculus  and so looking at the failure of those  uh and insufficiency of those uh  philosophies those moralities points to  i think a wider idea which is that  actually we all seem to occupy some  world that tries to have a local  consistency but draws in on different  traditions and different ideas when we  have to make concrete judgments and that  i think actually is not only uh the  natural human tendency it's also  probably the uh philosophically speaking  the the the the best description we can  give of how people behave in outline  although we can always go deeper so the  final thought would be does that mean  that our personal moralities are  unimprovable absolutely not we can  always be  thinking about our own practice thinking  about  the rules and norms of our society  thinking about the best public policy  from a moral perspective that is more  demanding perhaps than  we're used to in order to test it in  order to see how good it is and we can  apply the same standards to our own  lives and try to live better lives  ourselves at the same time as well  thank you okay julie bendel  well i'm interested in the type of  cultural relativism  as a feminist who campaigns to end male  violence against women and girls  where  if we're in the west or the global north  looking out at  cultures for example in the global south  where there's lots of  discussion about whether it's judgmental  to condemn female genital mutilation  forced or early marriage  and the like  and yet the only  culture that's relevant in this  discussion as far as i'm concerned is  patriarchy so the culture of patriarchy  that excuses  or even condones and encourages the  abuse of women by men in order to uphold  that that power  but  conversely when morality is discussed  within the parameters  of sex and obviously as a feminist i'm  very very  interested in how male sexual behavior  um  upholds the systems of patriarchy when  morality is discussed often it's  actually in order to condemn the likes  of me  we are those of us that that speak out  against rape um against harmful sexual  practices that are now sold as  liberating such as choking spitting  during sex other forms of abusive  practice such as coercing women to watch  violent pornography and re-enact scenes  in violent pornography were actually  seen or labeled as  moralists and what that means is  anti-sex  puritan  it's very directly an insult which then  stifles a discussion about what a true  moral compass would be  what morality truly is when we look at  sex and sexual practice for example  prostitution the act of a man because it  usually almost always is a man  paying for access to the inside of a  woman's body because it almost always is  a woman for one-sided sexual pleasure  um we're told that we are moralistic if  we campaign to end the sex trade and  wish to imagine a world without  prostitution and yet is it really moral  to speak about consent when the cash is  the consent when it's clear to that man  paying for sex that the woman does not  want sex with him or he wouldn't be  paying her  so interesting sorry to interrupt but  isn't it interesting that the word  moralistic is actually applied  as a criticism as it's a derogatory word  whereas it ought to mean being moral  absolutely and is it really moral i mean  you've hit the nail on the head marianne  is it really moral to  think to dehumanize and commodify women  to the point of where you can separate  orifices um to which you you know you  pay for access  when it's clear that this is a one-sided  um arrangement  similarly you know when we when we speak  about sexual morality well people then  will think  we'll go back to  for example um victorian morality which  is supposedly anti-sex but during that  time during for example between 1880 and  1914 where feminists were speaking about  men's sexual behavior that's harmful to  women those feminists campaigned against  the abuse and exploitation  of poor women and girls within the sex  trade and also campaigned to end rape in  marriage to to criminalize rape and  marriage so to me um  as opposed to to wrap up  morality  when we think about it in terms of  feminism and about the kind of campaigns  that feminists that aim to end male  violence  uh  are involved in morality has to be about  reshaping  men's sexual rights and looking  at the way that those rights become  wrongs when done to women  we have to be able to imagine a world  without prostitution without rape  without male violence  um as a moral ethical and political goal  and a utopian goal because utopia is  underrated um and there's nothing of  course that is uh moral in abusing women  okay thank you joe wolfe  let me start trying to answer this  question by  telling you a little bit  about my teacher g.a cohen um so he was  a marxist much of his life he was  brought up in fact in a jewish  marxist household in montreal  and he was aware very aware that most of  his beliefs he got from his mother  and his local community and he said that  had he been brought up  in a different family if he had been  brought up in a conservative catholic  family he probably would have had quite  different beliefs  so thank goodness he said i was brought  up in the right family  now in his case  he thought he had the correct moral  views  even though he got them from his family  and thought that if he'd been brought up  in a different family he had different  views the wrong ones  so  philosophically i find this such an  interesting  story because  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below or visit iai dot tv  for more debates and talks from the  world's leading thinkers on today's  biggest ideas  you
 

